      en: "The Osogovo Valley is a tectonic depression in western Bulgaria, lying at an elevation of 500-800 m between the Osogovo and Rila mountains. It is filled with alluvial deposits from the Struma River and its tributaries. The fertile soil supports intensive agriculture, including orchards and vineyards. The main town, Kyustendil, has a population of about 40,000 and is famous for its mineral springs since Roman times (ancient Pautalia). The valley also has a rich tradition of fruit growing, especially apples. Winters are cold (average -2°C in January) and summers warm (22°C in July).",
